# 📚 Today's Topic

**Variable-Length Positional Arguments – Introduction (`*args`)**

### 🌟 Why are we learning this?

Sometimes we don't know how many values a function will receive.
Instead of creating many different functions, we can write one function that accepts multiple values.

---

# 📖 Learn Before You Code

## What is it?

`*args` lets a function accept any number of positional values.
Python collects all the values into one group.
You can use a loop to work with each value.

## Why do programmers use it?

Imagine a shopping bag. Whether you put in two items or ten items, the same bag holds them all.

## Real-Life Example

A teacher may record attendance for a class with different numbers of students each day. One attendance sheet works for everyone.

## Important Points

- `*args` accepts multiple values.
- The `*` symbol is the important part.
- You can pass one value or many.
- The values can be processed using a loop.
- It makes functions reusable.

> 💡 **Remember:** `args` is just a common name—the `*` makes it special.

## Common Beginner Mistake

Forgetting to use `*` before the parameter name.

## Tiny Summary

`*args` allows one function to work with different numbers of inputs without changing the function.

